Multiple Sessions
Hostlog Client can be used to log messages from multiple hosts at once aka it can have separate session per different host at the same time. Every online command and related RPC API endpoint support having multiple sessions. In order to utilize this functionality, a configuration must be provided as described below. When a session configuration is used with --session-config option, all the other command line options will be ignored other than the output (--output or -o) option.

Session Configuration
The session configuration represents options of online commands and appconfig options. The content of a sample session configuration file is given below,

---
global_options:
  output: ~/hostlog_client/merged/
  enable_merged_outputs: false
  send_tdfs: true
  appconfig:
     output:
        directory:
        files:
          binary_capture_split_megabytes: 10240
     statistics:
        interval_seconds: 10
     # client: NOT ALLOWED TO BE OVERRIDDEN ON GLOBAL LEVEL

sessions:
  - VCU:
      host: 192.168.1.100
      port: 30401
      definitions: ~/vcu/tdf/tdf-20230808_112802604_vcu
      # topics and topic_config are mutually exclusive.
      topics: "2018:*"
      # topic_config: /path/to/config
      no_decode: true
      # decode_topics | no_decode_topics: ...
      to_binary: true
      to_hdf5: true
      to_message_server: true
      to_text: true
      max_messages: 1000
      # max_time_s | max_time_m | max_time_h : ...
      # max_kbytes | max_mbytes | max_gbytes : ...
      statistics: basic  # off | on | basic | advanced
      timeout: 5
      appconfig:
        client:  # THE ONLY APPCONFIG THAT CAN BE OVERRIDDEN IN INDIVIDUAL SESSIONS
          fetch_tdf_timeout_seconds: 10
  - ADPM:
      host: 192.168.1.101
      port: 30401
      to_hdf5: true
      topics: all
      definitions: ~/adpm/tdf/tdf-20230808_112802604_adpm
      appconfig:
        client: #THE ONLY APPCONFIG THAT CAN BE OVERRIDDEN IN INDIVIDUAL SESSIONS
          start_logging_timeout_seconds: 30
          fetch_tdf_timeout_seconds: 20
Global Options
global_options section contains global options for all the sessions. It contains the following options,

output - The output directory to be used. This directory will be used as the output directory for all the session output files
enable_merged_outputs - Whether to enable merging output files generated by the sessions. When enabled. binary, hdf5 and text output files will be merged together. Default is true.
send_tdfs - Whether to send topic definition files and related configuration information when logging messages, Default is true.
appconfig - Only output and statistics settings are allowed to be specified. Please refer appconfig for more information about this configuration
Notes:

This entire section is optional

The output directory can only be specified in this section

It is also possible to specify the output directory via the command line interface

The output directory override order

Default appconfig <== output value <== appconfig section <== CLI output
When merging of outputs is enabled, the messages collected in both sessions would be merged if both session has requested to create the supported output formats such as binary, hdf5 and text

For example,
global_options:
  enable_merged_outputs: true
sessions:
  - Session1:
      to_binary: true
      ...
  - Session2:
      to_binary: true
      to_hdf5: true
      ...
This will produce a single binary output containing all the messages from both sessions at <output directory>/bin/ and a single hdf5 output containing messages from Session2 host at <output directory>/logs/session2/<timestamp>-hostlog-messages.hdf5

client appconfig can not be overridden under this section

Sessions
The online command options can be specified as a list under session section

host - The IP address of the host. Default is 127.0.0.1

port - The host's port number. Default is 30401

definitions - The path to topic definition file(s). By default, topic definitions will be fetched before logging messages

topics - Topic subscriptions as a comma separated list

topic_config - The path to configuration file generated by frontend UI

no_decode - The flag to disable decoding of received messages. Defaults to false

decode_topics - The comma separated list of topics to decode

no_decode_topics - The comma separated list of topics to exclude from decoding. Defaults to false

to_binary - Enables binary output. The value could be a flag or path to output binary file. Defaults to false

to_hdf5 - Enables HDF5 output. The value could be a flag or path to output HDF5 file. Defaults to false

to_message_server - The flag to start the message server that can be used to retrieve messages via a socket connection. Default is false

to_text - Enables text output. The value could be a flag or path to output text file. Defaults to false

Stopping conditions for logging, all of these options are mutually exclusive

max_messages - The maximum number of messages to log

Time based stopping conditions

max_time_s - Maximum logging time in seconds
max_time_m - Maximum logging time in minutes
max_time_h - Maximum logging time in hours
Data size based stopping conditions

max_kbytes - Maximum number of kilobytes to log
max_mbytes - Maximum number of megabytes to log
max_gbytes - Maximum number of gigabytes to log
statistics - The type of statistics to enable. One of off, on, basic or advanced can be used. Default is off

timeout - The maximum time to wait before returning if the host is unavailable/unresponsive/unreachable. Default is 2 when fetching topic definitions and fetching CS manifest and 10 when logging messages.

appconfig - Only client settings are allowed to be specified. Please refer appconfig for more information about this configuration

Notes:

topics and topic_config options are mutually exclusive and either of them must be specified for a given session

decode_topics and no_decode_topics options are mutually exclusive

session section supports both list and dictionary format such as below,

  sessions:
    - Session1:
        ...
    - Session2:
        ...
  sessions:
    Session1:
      ...
    Session2:
      ...
It is possible to have configuration for only one session as well such as below (as if session-config had not been provided),

sessions:
  - VCU:
      ...
How to Use Hostlog Client with Multiple Sessions
The session configuration file can be used with --session-config option that is available with online commands such as below,

hostlog online fetch-cs-manifest --session-config /path/to/session_config.yml
hostlog online fetch-tdf --session-config /path/to/session_config.yml
hostlog online start-logging --session-config /path/to/session_config.yml
This will override the default output path or the output path specified in the session config

hostlog online start-logging --session-config /path/to/my_session_config.yaml --output /path/to/new/output
Minimal Examples
Note that it is not required to specify all the sections as specified above. Specify the values for the sections when the default value is not sufficient for your use-case.

Remote Procedure Call API (2.0)
Fetch-tdf

curl -X 'POST' \
  'http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/2.0/fetch-tdf' \
  -H 'accept: application/json' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
  "session_config": {
    "sessions": [
      {
        "HPA":
        {
            "host": "198.19.4.1",
            "port": 30401  // Not really required but added for clarity
        }
      },
      {
        "HPB":
        {
            "host": "198.19.4.49",
            "port": 30401  // Not really required but added for clarity
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}'
Start-logging

curl -X 'POST' \
  'http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/2.0/start-logging' \
  -H 'accept: application/json' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
  "session_config": {
    "sessions": [
      {
        "HPA":
        {
            "host": "198.19.4.1",
            "port": 30401,  // Not really required but added for clarity
            "topics": "all"
        }
      },
      {
        "HPB":
        {
            "host": "198.19.4.49",
            "port": 30401,  // Not really required but added for clarity
            "topics": "all"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}'
General Notes
The same session configuration can be used with different online commands without having to have multiple session configurations for each online command. Each online command would only use what is relevant to the specific command
The message server will output messages from both sessions regardless of the setting specified for enable_merged_outputs. If it is not required to have messages from both sessions, then disable message server option from the session where it does not require messages
This functionality can be accessed via Remote Procedure Call API (2.0) as well. It can be enabled by hostlog web start --api-version 2. The API documentation is served at http://127.0.0.1:5000/docs or http://127.0.0.1:5000/redoc
A UI for providing support in creating session configs is provided in the WEB UI. Start the web server with hostlog web start. Use a web browser to open http://127.0.0.1:5000/session-configs
When the sessions are configured to produce merged outputs, it is essential that the system has enough free memory in the shared memory. Otherwise, the application will exit with an exception
